================================================================================
!! RESULT INVALIDATED 2026-08-14, BY A FOLLOW-UP CHECK RUN AFTER THIS OUTPUT !!
================================================================================
The beta_D value above is an artifact of the T=1.0 horizon, NOT a property of
the model. sup_t Omega does not converge in T for the dispersive regulator at
nu = 0:

  N=4, profile P3, energy-conservation ceiling k_N^2 * E = 160.00
       T      nu=0.02       D=0.02   D as % of ceiling
       1       8.9691      30.7189            19.2%
       2      10.1035      49.8122            31.1%
       4      10.1035      72.1170            45.1%
       8      10.1035      72.1170            45.1%
      16      10.1035      82.9879            51.9%
      32      10.1035      96.0599            60.0%
      64      10.1035     105.8236            66.1%

The viscous column converges by T=2 and is stable to T=64. The dispersive
column is still climbing at T=64. Mechanism: dispersion is energy-neutral, so
with nu=0 the system has no attractor and the enstrophy keeps finding new
maxima, bounded only by k_N^2 * E -- which is a property of the TRUNCATION,
not of the dispersion.

Consequence: beta_nu is a model property; beta_D as measured here is a
property of the chosen horizon. They are not commensurable, so the
"DISJOINT -> distinguishable" conclusion above does not stand.

Recorded as OPEN ITEM O7 on the design memo. Do not cite the beta values above.
